# Adversarial Assessment of the Persistent-Corridor Mechanism

The previous mechanism is **not valid in its original unrestricted form**. Two of its key steps fail on a very small tree. However, those failures occur outside the regime \(q=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil\) relevant to the target statement, so they do not by themselves refute a suitably restricted version.

The correct verdict is therefore **Outcome C — Inconclusive**, with a much more precise lemma suggested below.

---

# Part I — Branching-Compensation Claim

## Claim A

A natural rigorous interpretation is:

> Given a \(q\)-round burning strategy maximizing the coverage profile, if a final unburned component has a boundary vertex of degree at least \(3\), then there is an equally good strategy whose final obstruction is no longer attached through a branching vertex.

This claim is **false in general**.

### [DIRECTLY FROM DATA] Smallest counterexample found

Take the 6-vertex double star

$$
T:
\qquad
u-v,
$$

with two leaves attached to \(u\) and two leaves attached to \(v\).

Thus

$$
\deg(u)=\deg(v)=3,
$$

and all four remaining vertices are leaves.

Its edge set can be written

$$
\{uv,ua,ub,vc,vd\}.
$$

The exact burning number is

$$
\boxed{b(T)=3}.
$$

Consider \(q=2\). After two rounds the maximum possible coverage is

$$
\boxed{5}
$$

of the six vertices.

Every maximum-coverage sequence is, up to symmetry, of the form

$$
(u,c)
$$

or

$$
(v,a),
$$

where the second source is a leaf attached to the *other* branching vertex.

For example, with sources

$$
(u,c),
$$

after two rounds the burned set is

$$
\{u,v,a,b,c\},
$$

leaving \(d\) unburned.

The unique boundary vertex of the remaining component is \(v\), and

$$
\deg(v)=3.
$$

There is no maximum-coverage two-round sequence whose final unburned component has only degree-\(\le2\) boundary.

### Why source rerouting fails

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

If the first source is \(u\), the second-round spread from \(u\) already burns

$$
v,a,b.
$$

The second source can add at most one additional vertex, so to achieve coverage 5 it must be placed on a leaf of the \(v\)-side.

But then the other \(v\)-leaf necessarily remains unburned.

Moving the second source to \(v\) is impossible because \(v\) is already burned at the beginning of round 2.

Moving it to \(a\) or \(b\) produces no additional gain, because those vertices are reached by the round-2 spread anyway.

Thus the branching vertex is not a dispensable boundary artifact. It is part of the timing obstruction.

### Important qualification

[DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

This double star has

$$
n=6,\qquad \lceil\sqrt6\rceil=3,
$$

while the failed strategy above uses only \(q=2\).

Therefore it is **not a counterexample to the original target statement**.

It is a counterexample to the unrestricted branching-compensation claim.

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The relevant version may still become true after imposing the actual-counterexample conditions

$$
q=\lceil\sqrt n\rceil,\qquad b(T)\ge q+1.
$$

The existing \(n\le19\) computation contains no such counterexample, so that restricted claim cannot currently be tested against an actual obstruction.

