# Part II — Two-Degree-2-Vertices-per-Layer

The statement

> each additional persistence layer requires two previously unused degree-2 vertices

is not yet mathematically defined.

The phrase "persistence layer" needs a formal object before the claim can be proved or disproved.

A natural interpretation is:

> one persistence layer = one additional round during which a particular residual unburned component survives.

Under that interpretation the double star is again fatal to an unrestricted statement.

### [DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

The double star has

$$
n_2=0,
$$

yet a one-step residual obstruction remains after an optimally covered two-round attempt.

Thus a rule of the form

$$
\text{persistence depth }k
\Longrightarrow
n_2\ge2k-1
$$

already fails at \(k=1\), under the natural interpretation.

The failure mechanism is exactly that the surviving region can be attached through a branching vertex rather than a degree-2 corridor.

### A second problem: charging overlap

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

Even if one restricts attention to degree-2 corridors, a naive temporal charging argument can double-count:

* the same degree-2 vertex may lie on the obstruction trace for more than one round;
* two residual components may be separated only after a common degree-2 path;
* a source placement can change the future obstruction trace.

Therefore the correct charging object must be **disjoint as a combinatorial object**, not merely "associated with" a round.

This is a major weakness in the original mechanism.

