# Part IV — A Better Potential Function

The original "number of degree-2 vertices consumed per round" is too coarse.

A more promising direction is a **dynamic active-tip path-packing potential**.

## Candidate potential

At time \(t\), let \(U_t\) be the unburned forest, and let the remaining radius budget be

$$
r=q-t.
$$

Identify "active tips" as residual leaves that cannot be reached from the already burned boundary within the remaining radius without using a new source.

For a family \(\mathcal P\) of vertex-disjoint paths joining active tips, define

$$
\Phi_t
=
\max_{\mathcal P}
\sum_{P\in\mathcal P}
|V(P)\cap V_2(T)|.
$$

Because the paths are vertex-disjoint,

$$
\boxed{\Phi_t\le n_2(T)}.
$$

This is the useful feature missing from the naive layer-counting argument.

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

A possible obstruction mechanism would then be:

$$
b(T)>q
$$

forces a sequence of residual states for which the active-tip packing potential stays positive for many rounds.

If one could establish that a \(q\)-round obstruction necessarily creates

$$
2q-1
$$

units of disjoint degree-2 path material, then the desired bound would follow.

This is still only a candidate mechanism.

### Why this is better than the original potential

It solves one specific defect:

> **No degree-2 vertex can be charged twice**, because the final charge is based on vertex-disjoint paths.

The remaining issue is much sharper:

> Why should a failed \(q\)-round sequence force a packing of total degree-2 size \(2q-1\)?

That is now a concrete question.

