# Part VI — Candidate Lemma 3: Ball-Overlap / Packing Lemma

This is a substantially different formulation.

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

Suppose \(T\) is a minimum-order tree with

$$
b(T)\ge q+1.
$$

Consider every possible \(q\)-ball covering with radii

$$
q-1,q-2,\ldots,0.
$$

The hypothesis is:

> Every such attempted covering has an unavoidable overlap/deficit region whose supporting tree paths contain at least \(2q-1\) degree-2 vertices.

The key point would be that the obstruction is not simply "a long path."

Rather,

$$
\boxed{
\text{too much unavoidable overlap}
\Longrightarrow
\text{too little independent coverage}
\Longrightarrow
\text{critical degree-2 path packing}.
}
$$

### Stress tests

**Paths.**

For a path, the balls can be positioned almost without waste, so no obstruction appears before the square threshold.

**Stars.**

Balls overlap heavily at the center, but branching creates huge simultaneous coverage, so overlap alone cannot explain failure.

**Balanced spiders.**

The long arms create competing coverage requirements and therefore give the strongest positive evidence.

### Weakness

The phrase "unavoidable overlap" still needs a quantitative definition.

Until there is a numerical deficit that can be bounded by \(n_2\), this remains a framework rather than a lemma.

