# Part VII — Why \(2q-1\) May Still Be Important

There is one numerical observation worth taking seriously.

### [DIRECTLY FROM DATA]

$$
2q-1=q^2-(q-1)^2.
$$

It is exactly the gap between consecutive squares.

This is also the number of vertices added when a path grows from

$$
(q-1)^2
$$

vertices to

$$
q^2
$$

vertices.

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

This may indicate that the threshold is tied to a **square-capacity argument** for burning rather than to a purely local corridor count.

That is potentially important because the original corridor mechanism tried to derive

$$
2q-1
$$

only as

$$
2(q-1)+1.
$$

The latter is numerically convenient, but there is currently no proof that nature should organize the obstruction into exactly \(q-1\) two-vertex charges plus one.

The square-gap interpretation may therefore be more fundamental.

However, there is not enough evidence to decide between these explanations.

