# Part XI — Proposed Proof Architecture

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

A plausible architecture is:

$$
b(T)\ge q+1
$$

$$
\Downarrow
$$

choose a minimum-order counterexample

$$
\Downarrow
$$

choose a lexicographically maximal failed \(q\)-round sequence

$$
\Downarrow
$$

extract the final unresolved tips/components

$$
\Downarrow
$$

prove that their critical connections can be represented by thread paths

$$
\Downarrow
$$

remove overlaps using minimality / exchange arguments

$$
\Downarrow
$$

obtain \(q-1\) disjoint paths carrying at least two degree-2 vertices each

$$
+\ 1\text{ terminal degree-2 vertex}
$$

$$
\Downarrow
$$

$$
n_2\ge2q-1.
$$

The crucial improvement is that the proof no longer requires the false statement

$$
\text{branching is always removable}.
$$

Instead, branching would have to be shown either:

1. removable **in a minimum actual-\(q\) counterexample**, or
2. incapable of participating in the critical path packing.

